In this project, clipper and clamper circuits were designed and simulated using LTspice to study waveform shaping techniques. The clipper circuit was implemented with diodes in series and parallel configurations to limit the amplitude of sinusoidal and square wave inputs at specific threshold levels, demonstrating how signals can be protected or conditioned by removing portions of the waveform. The clamper circuit was built using diode-capacitor networks to shift the entire waveform vertically, adding a DC level without altering its shape. Both positive and negative clampers were tested to observe upward and downward shifts of AC signals. Through simulation, the project highlighted practical applications of clippers in signal limiting and overvoltage protection, and clampers in signal restoration and voltage level adjustment. The LTspice analysis provided clear visualization of input and output waveforms, reinforcing theoretical concepts with practical circuit behavior.
